"Emendation of Bacteroidaceae and Butyrivibrio and Descriptions of Desulfomonas gen. nov. and Ten New Species in the Genera Desulfomonas, Butyrivibrio, Eubacterium, Clostridium, and Ruminococcus (https://www.microbiologyresearch.org/content/journal/ijsem/10.1099/00207713-26-2-238)\nciteturn54search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 50.6 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; # Emendation of Bacteroidaceae and Butyrivibrio and Descriptions of Desulfomonas gen. nov. and Ten New Species in the Genera Desulfomonas, Butyrivibrio, Eubacterium, Clostridium, and Ruminococcus Image: Free ... Int J Syst Evol Microbiol 26, 238 (1976); https://doi.org/10.1099/00207713-26-2-238\n\nInternational Journal of Systematic and Evolutionary Microbiology Volume 26, Issue 2\n\n# Emendation of Bacteroidaceae and Butyrivibrio and Descriptions of Desulfomonas gen. nov. and Ten New Species in the Genera Desulfomonas, Butyrivibrio, Eubacterium, Clostridium, and Ruminococcus Image: Free\n\n  * W. E. C MOORE^{1}, J. L. JOHNSON^{1} and L. V. HOLDEMAN^{1}\n  * View Affiliations Hide Affiliations\n\n^{1} Anaerobe Laboratory, Virginia Polytechnic Institute and State University, Blacksburg, Virginia 24061\n\n  * Published: 01 April 1976\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe family Bacteroidaceae is emended to include bacteria that are monotrichous or lophotrichous. The genus Butyrivibrio is emended to include lophotrichous bacteria. Desulfomonas, a new genus of nonmotile, nonsporeforming, gram-negative, anaerobic, sulfate-reducing rods, and 10 new species of anaerobes, Desulfomonas pigra, Butyrivibrio crossotus, Ruminococcus gnavus, R. lactaris, R. obeum, Eubacterium dolichum, E. hadrum, E. ramulus, E. siraeum, and Clostridium leptum, are described. These species have been isolated from human intestinal contents or from human feces. The type strains of these species are ATCC 29098, 29175, 29149, 29176, 29174, 29143, 29173, 29099, 29066, and 29065, respectively.\n\n  * Published Online: 01/04/1976\n\n© American Society for Microbiology, 1976\n\n1976-04-01\n\n2026-09-02\n\nEmendation of Bacteroidaceae and Butyrivibrio and Descriptions of Desulfomonas gen. nov. and Ten New Species in the Genera Desulfomonas, Butyrivibrio, Eubacterium, Clostridium, and Ruminococcus\n\nInt J Syst Evol Microbiol 26, 238 (1976); https://doi.org/10.1099/00207713-26-2-238--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTreponema pectinovorum sp. nov. Isolated from Humans with Periodontitis (https://openalex.org/W2005931515)\nciteturn54search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 43.1 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; # Treponema pectinovorum sp. nov. ... DOI: 10.1099/00207713-33-4-852 ... Year: 1983 ... Smibert — Virginia Tech (ROR) ... Burmeister — Virginia Commonwealth University (ROR)\n# Treponema pectinovorum sp. nov. Isolated from Humans with Periodontitis\n\nOpenAlex ID: W2005931515\n\nDOI: 10.1099/00207713-33-4-852\n\nType: article\n\nYear: 1983\n\nPublication date: 1983-10-01\n\nLanguage: en\n\n## Source\n\nName: International Journal of Systematic Bacteriology\n\nISSN-L: 0020-7713\n\nISSN: 0020-7713, 1070-6259, 1465-2102\n\nPublisher: Microbiology Society\n\n## Citation\n\nVolume: 33\n\nIssue: 4\n\nPages: 852–856\n\n## Article links\n\n  * Article website\n\n## Authors\n\n  * Robert M. Smibert — Virginia Tech (ROR)\n  * J. A. Burmeister — Virginia Commonwealth University (ROR)\n\n## Abstract\n\nA new species of Treponema, Treponema pectinovorum, which was isolated from supragingival and subgingival samples from patients with periodontitis, is described. This new anaerobic species requires rumen fluid or short-chain volatile fatty acids and a fermentable energy source. Pectin, polygalacturonic acid, glucuronic acid, and galacturonic acid are the only substrates fermented. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nClostridium scindens sp. nov., a Human Intestinal Bacterium with Desmolytic Activity on Corticoids (https://www.microbiologyresearch.org/content/journal/ijsem/10.1099/00207713-35-4-478?crawler=true)\nciteturn54search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 41.1 years ago; Crawled: last month; # Clostridium scindens sp. nov., a Human Intestinal Bacterium with Desmolytic Activity on Corticoids Image: Free ... Clostridium scindens sp. nov., an obligate anaerobe with desmolytic activity, was isolated from human fecal flora. ... The type strain is ATCC 35704 (Bokkenheuser strain 19).\n\nInternational Journal of Systematic and Evolutionary Microbiology Volume 35, Issue 4\n\n# Clostridium scindens sp. nov., a Human Intestinal Bacterium with Desmolytic Activity on Corticoids Image: Free\n\n  * G. N. MORRIS^{1}, J. WINTER^{1}, E. P. CATO^{2}, A. E. RITCHIE^{3} and V. D. BOKKENHEUSER^{1,*}\n  * View Affiliations Hide Affiliations\n\n^{1} Department of Pathology, St. Luke’s Hospital Center, New York, New York 10025 ^{2} Department of Anaerobic Microbiology, Virginia Polytechnic Institute and State University, Blacksburg, Virginia 24061 ^{3} The National Animal Disease Center, Ames, Iowa 50010\n\n*Corresponding author.\n\n  * Published: 01 October 1985\n\n## Abstract\n\nClostridium scindens sp. nov., an obligate anaerobe with desmolytic activity, was isolated from human fecal flora. The desmolase, not associated previously with any specific intestinal microorganism, cleaves the carbon-carbon bond of 17-hydroxylated corticoids at C17-C20, thereby converting them to androstans (C19 steroids). In primary cultures on sheep blood agar plates, C. scindens forms minute, nonhemolytic colonies. The gram-positive rods (0.5 to 0.7 by 1 to 2.5 μm) are slightly curved. The rare oval terminal spores (0.8 to 2.0 μm in diameter) are extremely difficult to demonstrate in Gram-stained smears. More than 40% of the cells are fimbriated. Neither a capsule nor flagella are present. d-Fructose, d-glucose, lactose, d-mannose, d-ribose, and d-xylose are fermented. The major fermentation products are acetic acid, ethanol, and hydrogen. The type strain is ATCC 35704 (Bokkenheuser strain 19).\n\n  * Published Online: 01/10/1985\n\nCopyright © 1985 International Union of Microbiological Societies\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSpecies: Treponema pectinovorum (https://lpsn.dsmz.de/species/treponema-pectinovorum)\nciteturn54search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Name: Treponema pectinovorum Smibert and Burmeister 1983 ... Linking: To permanently link to this page, use https://doi.org/10.83108/rn.785118[Button: Copy to clipboard]Link copied to clipboard\n\n# Species Treponema pectinovorum\n\n  * 🧫 (validly published under the ICNP)\n\nClassification: \n\nBacteria Pseudomonadati Spirochaetota Spirochaetia Spirochaetales Treponemataceae Treponema Treponema pectinovorum\n\nName: Treponema pectinovorum Smibert and Burmeister 1983\n\nCategory, proposed as: species, sp. nov.\n\nEtymology: pec.ti.no’vo.rum. N.L. neut. n. pectinum (pectin-), pectin; N.L. neut. adj. suff. -vorum (vor-), devouring; from L. v. voro (vor-), to devour; N.L. neut. adj. pectinovorum (pectinovorus - pectinovora - pectinovorum), pectin devouring, pectin destroying\n\nPronunciation (Latin), gender: tre-po-NE-ma pek-ti-NO-wo-rum, neuter\n\nType strain: ATCC 33768; VPI D-36; VPI D-36-DR-2\n\n16S rRNA gene: M71237 Analyse FASTA\n\nValid publication: Smibert RM, Burmeister JA. Treponema pectinovorum sp. nov. isolated from humans with periodontitis. Int. J. Syst. Bacteriol. 1983; 33:852-856.\n\nNomenclatural status: validly published under the ICNP\n\nTaxonomic status: correct name (and explicitly recommended for medical use)\n\nRisk group: 2\n\n  * Notes:\n    * 😷 The risk group for Belgium has been imported on 2024-02-05. The full classification is: risk group = 2, note = \"human pathogen\". — The risk group for Canada has been imported on 2024-02-27. The full classification is: risk group = 2, note = \"Animal classification RG: 1 - Security sensitive biological agent: No - Terrestrial animal pathogen under Canadian Food Inspection Agency authority: No - Containment level: Containment Level 2\". — The risk group for Swiss Confederation has been imported on 2024-02-01. The full classification is: risk group = 2. — The risk group for Germany has been imported on 2026-08-13. The full classification is: risk group = 2. — If in doubt, use the risk group given in the regulations for your country and, if these are not available, use the risk group given in the catalogue of the culture collection from which you have obtained or intend to obtain the strain.\n    * 🎓 Name mentioned 31 times in PubMed until 2026-06-11.\n    * 🙆 This name is on the List of Recommended Names for bacteria of medical importance (LoRN) as the name to be applied to this species. The name is on LoRN because of the recorded risk group of the species itself.\n    * 🧍 According to Bartlett et al. (2022), this species is an established human pathogen. Publication: Bartlett A, Padfield D, Lear L, Bendall R, Vos M. A comprehensive list of bacterial pathogens infecting humans. Microbiology (Reading) 2022; 168:0. 🇬🇧 (United Kingdom)\n\nParent taxon: Treponema Schaudinn 1905 (Approved Lists 1980)\n\nAssigned by: Smibert RM, Burmeister JA. Treponema pectinovorum sp. nov. isolated from humans with periodontitis. Int. J. Syst. Bacteriol. 1983; 33:852-856.\n\nLinking: To permanently link to this page, use https://doi.org/10.83108/rn.785118[Button: Copy to clipboard]Link copied to clipboard\n\nRecord number: 785118--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nJCM Catalogue (https://www.jcm.riken.jp/cgi-bin/jcm/jcm_keyword?AN=Treponema&BN=socranskii&CN=socranskii)\nciteturn54search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Treponema socranskii subsp. socranskii Smibert et al. 1984 ... Type strain [2914].\nMedium: 1000;  Temperature: 37°C; Anaerobic; Rehydration fluid: 663.\n# JCM Catalogue\n\n* * *\n\nTreponema socranskii subsp. socranskii Smibert et al. 198